    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePork loin is the budget cut here.\u003c/strong\u003e It shreds beautifully and usually costs less per pound than pre-made carnitas. Buy it when it goes on sale and freeze it for later.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eFresh citrus can beat the bottle.\u003c/strong\u003e When oranges and limes are cheap, fresh juice tastes brighter and often costs less than the bottled stuff. Roll them on the counter first to get more juice out.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned beans and corn stretch the meal.\u003c/strong\u003e Stock up when they hit a dollar or less per can. They add protein and bulk so a little pork feeds a lot more people."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot on a kitchen scale and write the number down. You will need it to portion evenly at the end.",
                    "Rub the pork loin all over with cumin, chili powder, onion powder, dried oregano, and a little salt and pepper. Set it in the slow cooker and add the minced garlic, the cinnamon sticks, lime juice, and fresh orange juice.",
                    "Cover and cook on low for 8 to 10 hours, or on high for 4 to 5 hours, until the pork is fork-tender.",
                    "About an hour before it is done, shred the pork right in the pot with two forks and let it soak up the citrusy juices.",
                    "For crispy edges, spread the shredded pork on a foil-lined baking sheet and broil for 8 to 10 minutes. Watch it closely so it browns without burning.",
                    "Cook the rice per the package. Warm and drain the black beans and the corn.",
                    "In your weighed pot, combine the rice, beans, corn, and crisped pork, then fold in the fat free cheddar so it melts into the warm mix."
